   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend chapter 479 of the laws of 2011, amending the tax law relating\nto establishing the taxpayer refund choice act and affirming the right of\nstate residents to receive personal income tax refunds by paper check and\nrequiring the department of taxation and finance to fully describe any debit\ncard or direct deposit program, in relation to extending the effectiveness\nthereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S6613",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6613-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §3, Chap 479 of 2011",
    "lawSection": "Taxation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6613\n\nSPONSOR: LIU\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend chapter 479 of the laws of 2011, amending the tax law\nrelating to establishing the taxpayer refund choice act and affirming\nthe right of state residents to receive personal income tax refunds by\npaper check and requiring the department of taxation and finance to\nfully describe any debit card or direct deposit program, in relation to\nextending the effectiveness thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo repeal and extend the taxpayer refund choice act for five years\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill amends the effective date provisions of Chapter\n479 of the Laws of 2011. It extends, for another five years, the expira-\ntion date of this 2011 law, that requires that regardless of any action\nby Department of Tax and Finance to establish a prepaid debit card or\ndirect deposit program for personal income tax refunds, taxpayers still\nbe entitled to choose to receive their refunds by paper check.\n\nSection two of the bill provides the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nEvery year millions of New Yorkers overpay their state and local taxes\non the wages they receive. These overpaid funds remain the rightful\nproperty of the taxpayers. Taxpayers are entitled to the timely refund\nof their overpayment and should be able to receive their refunds in a\nmanner that is most convenient, understandable and best suited to their\nindividual financial needs. A few years ago, the Tax Department imple-\nmented a prepaid debit card refund program. At the time, the Department\nwas considering discontinuing the ability of taxpayers to receive their\nrefund by paper check. However, many people are comfortable with paper\nchecks, and prefer to receive their refund in this manner.To ensure that\nconsumers would still have this choice, the Legislature passed a law\n(Chapter 479 of 2011) to allow consumers to choose whether to receive\ntheir refund by direct deposit, debit card or paper check. That law was\noriginally made subject to a five year sunset date, which was extended\nfor another five years by Chapter 456 of the Laws of 2015. This bill\nrecognizes that many people do not want to be forced into using debit\ncards, and would extend for an additional five years the right of\ntaxpayers to choose how they want to receive their refund.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone. This bill continues the existing refund payment options for\ntaxpayers.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
